---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'How do I compress a PDF to 200 KB?', a: 'Choose the PDF, type 200, set the unit to KB and press Compress. The tool tries several resolutions and quality levels and keeps the best one that fits.' },
  { q: 'Is my PDF uploaded to a server?', a: 'No. The file is opened and rebuilt inside your browser. It never leaves your device, which matters for certificates and ID documents.' },
  { q: 'Why can I no longer select the text?', a: 'To make the file small enough, each page is turned into a compressed picture. That keeps the page looking the same but removes selectable text. Keep your original for any use that needs it.' },
  { q: 'What if the target is too small?', a: 'The tool shows the smallest file it could make. Remove pages you do not need, or ask whether the form allows a larger limit.' },
  { q: 'Can it open a password-protected PDF?', a: 'No. Remove the password in your PDF viewer first, then compress the unlocked copy.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout slug="compress-pdf-to-specific-size" faqs={faqs}>
  {/* AUTHOR REVIEW: lead paragraph, drafted by AI. */}
  <p slot="lead">
    Need to compress a PDF to a specific size for an upload form? Choose your file, enter the limit in KB or MB, and
    this tool rebuilds the PDF in your browser until it fits. Nothing is uploaded.
  </p>

  <div slot="tool">
    <div class="field">
      <label for="pc-file">Choose a PDF file</label>
      <input id="pc-file" type="file" accept="application/pdf,.pdf" />
    </div>
    <div class="row two">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="pc-size">Target size (maximum)</label>
        <input id="pc-size" type="number" min="1" step="any" inputmode="decimal" value="200" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="pc-unit">Unit</label>
        <select id="pc-unit"><option value="KB">KB</option><option value="MB">MB</option></select>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="field">
      <span class="label">Quick targets</span>
      <div class="chips">
        <button type="button" class="chip" data-pc data-size="100" data-unit="KB" aria-pressed="false">100 KB</button>
        <button type="button" class="chip" data-pc data-size="200" data-unit="KB" aria-pressed="true">200 KB</button>
        <button type="button" class="chip" data-pc data-size="500" data-unit="KB" aria-pressed="false">500 KB</button>
        <button type="button" class="chip" data-pc data-size="1" data-unit="MB" aria-pressed="false">1 MB</button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="btn-row"><button type="button" id="pc-go" class="btn" disabled>Compress PDF</button></div>
    <progress id="pc-prog" value="0" max="1" hidden></progress>
    <p id="pc-status" class="status" role="status" aria-live="polite">Choose a PDF to begin.</p>
    <div id="pc-result" class="result" hidden>
      <div id="pc-out" aria-live="polite"></div>
      <div class="btn-row"><button type="button" id="pc-dl" class="btn" disabled>Download compressed PDF</button></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* AUTHOR REVIEW: how-to, worked example and notes drafted by AI. */}
  <h2>How to compress a PDF to a specific size</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose the PDF from your device. Scans and photo-heavy files shrink the most.</li>
    <li>Type the maximum size from your form and pick KB or MB, or tap a quick target.</li>
    <li>Press Compress PDF and wait. Larger files take longer because every page is redrawn.</li>
    <li>Read the final size shown, then download the new file.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p class="worked">
    You scanned a four-page certificate and the PDF is 3.4 MB, but the form accepts up to 500 KB. Choose the file, tap
    500 KB and press Compress. The tool first tries a high-quality setting, sees that the file is still too big, then
    steps down until it lands on something like 470 KB. The pages look nearly the same on screen, and you upload the
    new file.
  </p>
  <h2>What this tool does and does not do</h2>
  <p>
    To reach a size you choose, the tool turns each page into a compressed JPEG picture and builds a fresh PDF. That is
    why it can hit a target reliably, and also why text is no longer selectable afterwards. Always keep your original
    file. The tool never knows your exam rules, so use the limit written in your official notification.
  </p>
  <script>import '../../scripts/pdf-compress.js';</script>
</ToolLayout>
